# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FinchHive Frontend — environment variables
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Quick start:
#   cp .env.example .env
#   (then fill in the values below and save)
#
# `.env` is git-ignored (see .gitignore's `.env*` rule) — your real secrets
# never get committed. This file (.env.example) has NO secrets, only
# documentation, and is deliberately excluded from that ignore rule
# (`!.env.example`) so it's checked into git for every developer to see.
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID / R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY ─────────────────────────────────
#
# What these are for:
#   scripts/seed-local-r2.mjs (run via `npm run seed:r2`) uses these to
#   mirror real images/videos from the private "finchhive" R2 bucket into
#   your local emulated bucket, so `wrangler pages dev` (see README.md §2)
#   can actually serve /api/... images instead of 404-ing. Without this,
#   local dev has an empty bucket and every image request fails.
#
# What these are NOT:
#   - NOT your Cloudflare account password.
#   - NOT the `wrangler login` browser session (that's separate OAuth and is
#     only used for `wrangler pages dev` itself, not for this script).
#   - NOT a Cloudflare "Global API Key" — never use that here.
#   These are S3-compatible credentials tied to one scoped R2 API token.
#
# ── How to create your own token (about 1 minute, one-time) ──
#
#   1. Log in to https://dash.cloudflare.com with the account this project
#      belongs to.
#   2. Left sidebar → R2 Object Storage → Overview.
#   3. Click "Manage API tokens" (top-right of that page — Cloudflare
#      occasionally renames/relocates this button; look for anything
#      mentioning "API tokens" on the R2 Overview page if it's moved).
#   4. Click "Create API token" / "Create User API Token".
#   5. Permissions — pick ONE:
#        - "Object Read & Write"  -> lets you both seed local dev AND
#          upload/delete assets from the CLI (see README.md §3). Use this
#          if you'll also be managing bucket contents.
#        - "Object Read only"     -> enough for `npm run seed:r2` alone.
#          Safer default if you only ever pull assets down, never push.
#   6. Under "Specify bucket(s)", scope it to just: finchhive
#      (always scope to one bucket — never create an account-wide token
#      unless you have a specific reason to).
#   7. Click "Create User API Token" / "Create Access Key".
#   8. The result screen shows THREE values — you only need two of them:
#        Token value          -> a Cloudflare API token. NOT used by this
#                                 project. You can ignore/discard it.
#        Access Key ID         -> copy into R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID below
#        Secret Access Key     -> copy into R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY below
#
#      ⚠️  The Secret Access Key is shown ONLY ONCE, at creation time. If
#      you navigate away before copying it, it cannot be recovered — you'd
#      have to delete that token and create a new one.
#
#   9. Paste both values below (in your real `.env` file, not this example),
#      save, then run:
#        npm run seed:r2
#
# ── Security ──
#   - Treat these two values exactly like a password: never paste them into
#     chat, a ticket, a screenshot, Slack, or a commit.
#   - If either value is ever exposed somewhere it shouldn't be, treat it as
#     compromised immediately: go to R2 → Manage API tokens → delete that
#     token, then create a fresh one and update your local `.env`.
#   - Blast radius is intentionally small: a token scoped to "finchhive"
#     with Object-level permissions can only read/write objects inside that
#     one bucket — it cannot touch any other bucket, Worker, DNS record, or
#     account setting.
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=


# ── CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What this is for:
#   Pins every Wrangler command run in this repo (e.g. `wrangler pages dev`,
#   `wrangler r2 object get/put ... --remote`) to this one Cloudflare account.
#   Without it, anyone whose login has access to more than one Cloudflare
#   account gets "More than one account available but unable to select one
#   in non-interactive mode" on any `--remote` command (confirmed).
#
# Why it's here and not in wrangler.toml:
#   This used to live in wrangler.toml, but Cloudflare Pages' config
#   validation rejects it there:
#     ✘ Configuration file for Pages projects does not support "account_id"
#   Pages projects get their account binding from the Git connection /
#   dashboard instead, so putting it in wrangler.toml breaks every deploy.
#   Wrangler reads the CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID environment variable
#   automatically, so setting it here achieves the same pinning locally
#   without touching wrangler.toml.
#
# Is this a secret?
#   No. An account ID is not sensitive — it's visible in the dashboard URL
#   and on the R2/Workers overview pages to anyone with account access. It's
#   safe to commit the real value here in .env.example (unlike the R2 keys
#   above, which must stay in your local .env only).
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=3b858374d9a5c95a61d0ac0a5c4ca244

# ── NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What this is for:
#   scripts/generate-sitemap.js (runs automatically as part of `npm run
#   build`, including production builds) uses this as the base URL for
#   every <loc> entry it writes into public/sitemap.xml, and for the
#   Sitemap: line in public/robots.txt.
#
# This is NOT a secret, just the production domain. The default below is
# almost certainly correct — only change it if you're deliberately testing
# sitemap generation against a different domain (e.g. a staging URL).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://finchhive.com
